### What is an IAM Role and why does it exist?

An IAM Role is an identity you assume temporarily to get short-lived credentials from STS. Unlike an IAM User, a Role has no password and no permanent access key. It exists because permanent credentials are dangerous: they do not expire, they can leak, and if they do, an attacker has durable access until a human manually rotates them. A Role eliminates that risk by issuing credentials that expire automatically.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sts.StsClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sts.model.GetCallerIdentityRequest;

public class CheckRoleIdentity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StsClient sts = StsClient.create();
        var identity = sts.getCallerIdentity(
                GetCallerIdentityRequest.builder().build());
        System.out.println("Account: " + identity.account());
        System.out.println("ARN: " + identity.arn());
        // If running on EC2 with a Role, this shows the assumed role ARN
    }
}
```

### How does an EC2 instance assume a Role?

When the settlement service runs on EC2, you attach an instance profile that wraps a Role. The AWS SDK's default credential provider chain automatically fetches temporary credentials from the instance metadata endpoint (`169.254.169.254`), uses them, and refreshes them before they expire. You write zero credential-handling code.

The metadata endpoint is available only from inside the instance, and IMDSv2 requires a session token to access it, adding another layer of protection.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.DefaultCredentialsProvider;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.regions.Region;

public class SettlementService {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // On EC2 this picks up the Role's temporary credentials automatically
        // No access key in code, no secret in config files
        S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder()
                .region(Region.AP_SOUTH_1)
                .credentialsProvider(DefaultCredentialsProvider.create())
                .build();

        var objects = s3.listObjectsV2(b -> b.bucket("nbi-customer-statements"));
        objects.contents().forEach(obj ->
                System.out.println("Statement: " + obj.key()));
    }
}
```

### How does cross-account access work with Roles?

National Bank of India has a production account and an analytics account. The analytics account must read production's settlement data. Instead of copying production access keys into the analytics account, you create a Role in production whose trust policy allows the analytics account to assume it. The analytics job calls `assumeRole` and gets temporary production credentials scoped to exactly what it needs.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sts.StsClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sts.model.AssumeRoleRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.AwsSessionCredentials;
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.StaticCredentialsProvider;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.regions.Region;

public class CrossAccountAnalytics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StsClient sts = StsClient.create();
        var assumed = sts.assumeRole(AssumeRoleRequest.builder()
                .roleArn("arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/ProdStatementReaderRole")
                .roleSessionName("analytics-daily-report")
                .build());
        var tempCreds = assumed.credentials();

        // Use temporary production credentials from analytics account
        S3Client prodS3 = S3Client.builder()
                .region(Region.AP_SOUTH_1)
                .credentialsProvider(StaticCredentialsProvider.create(
                        AwsSessionCredentials.create(
                                tempCreds.accessKeyId(),
                                tempCreds.secretAccessKey(),
                                tempCreds.sessionToken())))
                .build();
        System.out.println("Reading production statements with temp creds");
    }
}
```

### How does Lambda use Roles?

A Lambda function has an execution role. When the function is invoked, Lambda assumes that role automatically and makes the temporary credentials available through environment variables that the SDK reads. You never see the credentials in code.

```java
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.Context;
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.RequestHandler;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import java.util.Map;

public class StatementProcessor implements RequestHandler<Map<String, Object>, String> {
    // Lambda assumes the execution role automatically
    private final S3Client s3 = S3Client.create();

    @Override
    public String handleRequest(Map<String, Object> event, Context context) {
        // Temporary credentials from the execution role – no keys anywhere
        s3.getObject(b -> b.bucket("nbi-customer-statements").key("stmt-2024.pdf"));
        return "processed";
    }
}
```

### Why must application code never embed long-lived keys?

An access key is a two-part secret (key ID and secret key) that does not expire on its own. If it lands in a Git commit, a Docker image layer, a log line, or a stack trace, it is compromised forever until someone notices and rotates it. Attackers scan public repositories for exactly these strings within seconds of a push. Temporary credentials from STS cut the blast radius to the token's lifetime, usually one hour.

```java
public class WhyNoEmbeddedKeys {
    // WRONG: permanent secret in code – breach waiting to happen
    // If this file is committed to Git, the key is exposed forever
    static final String ACCESS_KEY = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE";
    static final String SECRET_KEY = "wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY";

    // CORRECT: no secrets in code at all
    // On EC2: instance profile provides temporary credentials
    // On Lambda: execution role provides temporary credentials
    // Locally: aws sso login or named profile
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        S3Client s3 = S3Client.create();  // SDK finds creds automatically
    }
}
```

### What is federation and how does it use Roles?

Federation means users log in through an external identity provider such as Active Directory, Okta, or Google Workspace, and then assume an IAM Role. They never get a permanent IAM User at all. AWS IAM Identity Center (formerly SSO) manages this flow, mapping identity-provider groups to IAM Roles so that when Rajesh logs in through the bank's Active Directory, he automatically assumes the Developer role with the right permissions.

```text
Rajesh → Bank Active Directory → IAM Identity Center → Assume DeveloperRole
         (authentication)         (federation)          (temporary credentials)
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

A Role's trust policy determines who can assume it. The trust policy names the trusted principal, which can be an AWS service (EC2, Lambda), an AWS account, a federated identity provider, or a specific IAM User or Role.

Session duration is configurable between 1 and 12 hours, but role chaining (when one Role assumes another Role) caps the session at 1 hour regardless of the configured maximum.

A Role can have up to 10 managed policies attached and one inline policy. The total size of all policies on a Role cannot exceed 10,240 characters.

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {
      "Service": "ec2.amazonaws.com"
    },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is embedding access keys in source code or environment variables committed to Git. The fix is to use Roles for all machine access.

The second mistake is giving a Role broader permissions than the workload needs. Even though the credentials are temporary, an overly permissive Role still lets a compromised service do too much during the token lifetime.

The third mistake is not using IMDSv2 on EC2. IMDSv1 allows any process on the instance to read credentials with a simple HTTP GET. IMDSv2 requires a session token, which blocks common SSRF attacks.

```java
public class RoleMistakes {
    // WRONG: Role with admin access
    // { "Effect": "Allow", "Action": "*", "Resource": "*" }
    // Even temporary credentials with full admin are dangerous

    // CORRECT: Role scoped to exactly what the service needs
    // { "Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
    //   "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-customer-statements/*" }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer enforces IMDSv2 on all instances, uses permission boundaries to cap what any Role in a development account can do, and monitors with IAM Access Analyzer for unused permissions. They know that role chaining caps sessions at one hour, that `aws sso login` is preferred over long-lived keys even for local development, and that credential reports and Access Analyzer findings should be reviewed regularly.

They also know that STS endpoints are regional and recommend using the regional STS endpoint for lower latency and higher availability rather than the global endpoint.

```bash
# Enforce IMDSv2 on launch – blocks SSRF credential theft
aws ec2 run-instances \
  --metadata-options HttpTokens=required,HttpEndpoint=enabled \
  --image-id ami-0abcd1234 \
  --instance-type m6i.large
```
